  "prompt_text": "The figure compares four surface-design strategies for controlling bubble behavior during boiling. In all panels, white circles represent vapor bubbles on a submerged heated surface, and arrows indicate bubble departure away from the surface and liquid replenishment toward it.\n\nThe main idea is that engineered surfaces can tune where bubbles nucleate, how easily they detach, and how well fresh liquid is supplied. A “biphilic” surface combines hydrophilic and hydrophobic regions, apparently using contrasting wetting patches so bubbles form at preferred spots while nearby wetting areas help liquid rewet the surface. A “biconductive” surface instead varies thermal conductivity, with low- and high-conductivity regions guiding bubble formation and heat transport. A “channelled” surface adds recessed grooves or trenches that seem to provide protected pathways for liquid flow and bubble management, promoting repeated nucleation in structured cavities. Finally, a “surfactant-tunable” surface shows an active switch: with surfactant-like molecules present, the surface has low surface energy and boiling is on, while removing or changing that chemistry raises surface energy and turns boiling off.\n\nOverall, the figure emphasizes that both chemistry and topography can be patterned to optimize boiling by balancing vapor generation with liquid resupply.",
